<p>
  Field biologists are very good at snapshots. On
  <a href="/research/lizard-island/">Lizard Island</a> we catch every anole twice a
  year, measure it, mark it and let it go, and from those snapshots we can measure
  natural selection with real precision. But what happens in between (the months a
  lizard spends moving through the canopy, defending a territory, finding a mate,
  or sitting out a cold night) has stayed almost entirely out of view. And watching
  a lizard does not solve the problem: it watches you back, and its behavior
  changes the moment an observer arrives.
</p>
<p>
  Wearable sensors close that gap. A tiny tag, fitted to a free-living lizard like
  a backpack, records movement, behavior, physiology and interactions passively,
  around the clock, with nobody standing nearby. Indeed, these are data types that
  eco-evolutionary field studies have simply not had before.
</p>

<h2>Why put a backpack on a lizard?</h2>
<p>
  Because behavior is the hinge between ecology and evolution. Natural selection
  acts on what animals do, not only on what they look like, yet most studies of
  selection in the wild measure morphology and survival and infer everything in
  between. Does a longer-legged anole survive because it runs faster, or because
  it uses different perches? Do individuals in the same population use their
  habitat in consistently different ways, and does selection care? Sensors let us
  ask these questions directly, linking the behavior of individual lizards to
  their survival and reproduction, and in doing so connecting process to pattern.
</p>
<p>
  The same logic applies to thermal ecology. Our work on green anoles suggests that
  behavior, rather than physiology, is what has allowed a lizard of tropical origin
  to push north into temperate Tennessee and North Carolina (see the
  <a href="/miami-lizards/green-anole/">green anole guide</a>). Sensors that record
  where and when a lizard moves through the winter let us test that idea on
  individual animals, as it happens.
</p>

<h2>Built at Georgia Tech</h2>
<p>
  Georgia Tech is one of the best engineering and computer science universities in
  the world, and we are an evolutionary ecology lab inside it. Building our own
  wearable sensors is one of the clearest ways we take advantage of that. In 2026
  Georgia Tech funded the next phase of the program, together with our colleagues
  Ben Freeman and Emily Weigl in the School of Biological Sciences. The campus
  itself has become our test bed: we are fitting green anoles on the Georgia Tech
  campus in Atlanta with ultra-lightweight trackers, and extending the same
  approach to butterflies and birds.
</p>
<p>
  A track is only as useful as the map underneath it. Our annual terrestrial LiDAR
  scans of Lizard Island record the location and size of every branch on the
  island, so a tagged lizard's movements can, in principle, be placed on the
  branches it actually used. We expect that pairing the two will let us measure how
  individuals use their structural habitat at a resolution no field study of
  selection has had, revealing which parts of the environment selection is acting
  through. The data streams are large, which is why we write our own
  <a href="/software/">software</a> to process them.
</p>
<p>
  We are confident that instrumenting organisms in the wild is the future of
  ecology and evolution. If you want to put backpacks on lizards (they look
  adorable, obvs), <a href="/join/">get in touch</a>.
</p>
